.\" join.1 -- maux's join(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as cksum.1/paste.1.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/JOIN.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/JOIN.
.TH JOIN 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
join \- relational join of two sorted files on a common field
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
[
.B \-a
.I n
|
.B \-v
.I n
] [
.B \-e
.I string
] [
.B \-o
.I list
] [
.B \-t
.I char
] [
.B \-1
.I n
] [
.B \-2
.I n
]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Join
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
each already sorted on its own join field (field 1 by default,
.B \-1
and
.B \-2
select a different field per side), and writes one line per
matching pair: the join field, then
.IR file1 's
other fields, then
.IR file2 's
other fields. It does not sort and does not check.
.PP
The default field separator is a run of blanks, leading blanks
ignored;
.B \-t
.I char
makes
.I char
the separator on both input and output, and every field it bounds
\(em an empty one included \(em counts.
.B \-a
.I n
adds
.IR n 's
own unpaired lines to the output;
.B \-v
.I n
prints
.B only
.IR n 's
unpaired lines, in place of the normal output.
.B \-o
.I list
is a comma-separated list of
.BI m . n
field selectors (or the bare digit
.B 0
for the join field) choosing and ordering the output fields; an
item naming a side that has no line for the current record prints
empty, replaced by
.B \-e
.I string
when given (the XCU/GNU rule: applied only through
.BR \-o ;
BSD's own
.B \-e
differs).
.RB ( \-1 / \-2 )
field numbers and
.B \-o
selectors are single digits in this build.
.PP
.RB "A " \-
names standard input for either operand (not both at once).
.SH FILES
/usr/man/JOIN  this page
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 1 if a file could not be opened; 2 on
a usage error.
.SH BUGS
None known.
